{:itonami.blueprint/id "cloud-itonami-isic-6209"
 :itonami.blueprint/name "Governed IT Managed-Services Ticket-Routing Actor"
 :itonami.blueprint/isic-rev5 "6209"
 :itonami.blueprint/domain :technology/it-managed-services
 :itonami.blueprint/license "AGPL-3.0-or-later"
 :itonami.blueprint/social-impact [:least-privilege-access-control :security-incident-integrity]
 :itonami.blueprint/governor :ticket-governor
 :itonami.blueprint/robotics false
 :itonami.blueprint/required-technologies [:identity :forms :audit-ledger]
 :itonami.blueprint/optional-technologies [:dmn :bpmn]
 :itonami.blueprint/status :public-oss
 :itonami.blueprint/maturity :implemented
 :itonami.blueprint/implemented-slice
 "TicketRouter-LLM sealed into a single :advise node, always routed through
 an independent TicketGovernor (src/itsupport/policy.cljk) and a 0->3
 rollout-phase gate, via a langgraph-clj StateGraph (src/itsupport/
 operation.cljc) with an append-only audit ledger and a MemStore +
 DatomicStore (langchain.db) SSoT proven to satisfy the same store
 contract test. Governed operations: :ticket/route, :disclosure/query,
 :dispute/request. Eight governor checks -- five HARD (rbac,
 access-tier-clearance-gate, security-incident-misrouting-gate,
 source-provenance-gate, licensed-disclosure) and three SOFT/
 always-escalate (confidence floor, sla-breach-imminent gate, dispute
 requests). access-tier-clearance-gate and security-incident-misrouting-
 gate are domain-unique: the former is a NIST SP 800-53 AC-6
 least-privilege-style three-tier clearance check (standard/elevated/
 privileged) requiring a technician's access-tier to meet or exceed a
 ticket's required tier; the latter requires a :security-incident-
 category ticket to route only to a technician holding at least one of
 three real named incident-response certifications (GIAC GCIH, CISSP,
 CHFI) from a closed R0 catalog. A ticket within 60 minutes of SLA breach
 always escalates to a human regardless of confidence, and a dispute
 request never auto-resolves at any phase. This actor routes tickets to
 already-contracted technicians only -- the store holds no field for
 payroll, timesheet approval, or employer-of-record status, and it never
 bypasses the TicketGovernor for production routing or disclosures."}
